PORT=3001
HOST=127.0.0.1
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
DATA_DIR=.data
# Production needs HTTPS, a TLS reverse proxy and the launch gates in docs/engineering.
NODE_ENV=development
# .env is loaded by server/index.ts. Existing environment values take precedence.
# Set APP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:3001 for the combined local built-client server.
# NODE_ENV=production requires an explicit HTTPS APP_ORIGIN.
# Optional email delivery. Set a verified sender and real SMTP credentials to enable
# password recovery and opted-in reminders while the browser is closed.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=
# Port 465 uses TLS immediately; other ports require STARTTLS. No insecure fallback.
# Without SMTP, development saves emails to DATA_DIR/outbox (read with npm run outbox).
# Set MAIL_TRANSPORT=none to switch email off entirely; production always needs SMTP.
# MAIL_TRANSPORT=outbox
# Browser push reminders. Keys are generated into DATA_DIR/vapid.json when unset; production should
# set a fixed pair (base64url P-256 public point and private scalar) and a contact subject.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=Continue from the previous read-only audit. Do not repeat the Git history, old-brand grep, deployment identifier, or tracked-secret audit unless a finding directly affects the application changes.

This task is an APPLICATION WORKFLOW AND DEPENDENCY AUDIT ONLY. Do not edit or create any files.

Our goal is to finish a smaller, reliable DocSathi college project within one hour. Keep the existing frontend, backend, and database architecture. Do not replace database persistence with browser localStorage.

Trace the actual end-to-end implementation and report the exact files, functions, API routes, database tables, and UI components for:

1. Registration, login, logout, session persistence, protected routes, and authorization between users.
2. Creating or managing an application/workspace and saving checklist changes.
3. Uploading, listing, retrieving, and deleting documents using the existing backend and private storage.
4. The most reliable document-analysis or comparison workflow.
5. Generating and viewing a readiness report.

For each workflow, state whether it is verified working, partially implemented, dependent on an external service, or not yet verified. Do not infer that a feature works merely because its code exists.

Then trace the dependencies of these optional capabilities:
- SMTP/email delivery and password recovery;
- email reminders and browser push notifications;
- guardian approval and withdrawal;
- scheduled/off-site backups;
- monitoring and metrics;
- virus scanning;
- parser/OS sandboxing;
- any production-only integrations required by the current startup configuration.

For every optional capability, recommend KEEP, DISABLE, or REMOVE, with the reason and specific dependencies. Prefer disabling optional functionality cleanly over deleting complex code when removal risks breaking core workflows. Identify all user-facing buttons, navigation entries, API endpoints, startup checks, environment requirements, tests, and documentation that would become inconsistent if a capability is disabled.

Constraints:
- Preserve login, authorization, database persistence, private file storage, uploads, and at least one complete document workflow.
- Keep security validation and access controls for the features that remain.
- Do not change .gitignore or .env.example.
- Do not add dependencies, APIs, services, or new features.
- Do not change database schemas, persistent identifiers, storage formats, backup formats, or compatibility-sensitive names unless absolutely necessary.
- Preserve historical authorship information, third-party licenses, and acknowledgements.
- Do not change Git history, remotes, or repository configuration in this task.
- Do not make any edits.

Return:
A. A concise core-workflow dependency map.
B. A prioritized list of exact, minimal code changes for the next step.
C. A list of functionality that must remain untouched.
D. A smoke-test checklist covering login, authorization, persistence, uploads, and the chosen document workflow.

We will use your findings to approve the implementation changes in the next prompt.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:support@example.com
# Behind a hosting proxy (Caddy, Render, a load balancer): the number of trusted proxy hops.
TRUST_PROXY=
# Virus scanning before inspection, with a clamd service (docker compose --profile scan).
CLAMAV_HOST=
CLAMAV_PORT=3310
# Production requires scanning and real OS isolation; no unsandboxed fallback.
# Linux needs bubblewrap, util-linux and working unprivileged user namespaces.
# Development defaults to process mode; production defaults to bubblewrap.
INSPECTION_MODE=
# Scheduled encrypted backups. Copy the directory off the server as well.
BACKUP_DIR=
BACKUP_PASSWORD=
BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=24
BACKUP_KEEP=7
# Optional automated encrypted off-site repository (S3/SFTP/HTTPS REST/etc.).
# Initialise once with npm run backup:offsite -- init. Credentials stay outside Git.
# A local directory is useful for tests, but does not survive losing this server.
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=
RESTIC_PASSWORD=
# Alternatively RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE can point to a private mounted secret.
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are used for an S3 repository.
# Protected operational metrics. Use the same random token in the Prometheus secret file.
# No applicant can access metrics using their normal account session.
MONITOR_TOKEN=
